      ":question/id": "78b6b2c2-5471-5e63-b66b-2a0735954c6f",
      ":question/problem": "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{8}{3x}?$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "954caf8b-9934-565d-a4fa-82407c2fbda7",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "1128c393-5902-5358-a380-a5b669d52fb7",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(|x|+K)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "b488a450-d2a2-5b5d-84b8-00e65cdb30af",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|x|)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "d8a7b70a-4b9b-550e-91d2-45d16a83833e",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8|x|+K)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "3bc3fe71-9084-53d3-8a1e-dd9e5151f79b",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{8}{3}K|x|)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "7efc64cc-9bf7-549e-846d-292319417c1c",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8K|x|)"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "b488a450-d2a2-5b5d-84b8-00e65cdb30af"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{8}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
