      ":question/id": "b368bf60-b73f-5866-b7d7-8b1c970a3ddf",
      ":question/problem": "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}?$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "5e8aeaad-d950-5507-bf73-2a6bbc6750ae",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "bab82d98-45b5-5b4e-af2c-f66dde9290d4",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln\u2061(K|x|)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "37d9150e-0ab4-5b2c-8571-96d015b14b81",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "1-\\frac{3}{2x}+ln\u2061(3K|x|)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "0bcc4034-8ad0-5962-924f-f449063045a0",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "x-\\frac{3}{2x}+3ln\u2061(K|x|)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "fdf00b35-0ed9-5014-8fbc-e7349b5def97",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+ln\u2061(3K|x|)"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "b4873607-8bfd-53aa-b764-be29e33835e0",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln\u2061(K|x|)"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "b4873607-8bfd-53aa-b764-be29e33835e0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+3ln\u2061|x|+\\frac{9{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+3ln\u2061|x|-\\frac{9}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=3ln\u2061K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3ln\u2061|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln\u2061|x|+3ln\u2061K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3(ln\u2061|x|+ln\u2061K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln\u2061(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard"
    },
